// PROTECTED graded final MCQ: Collections & Streams. Not for learners or Git.
$CATEGORY: collections-streams/final-mcq

::cs-final-q01::[html]<p>A support tool reuses one <code>ArrayDeque</code> for urgent and normal tickets. What happens when this code runs?</p><pre><code>ArrayDeque&lt;String&gt; d \= new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();&\#10;d.push("a");&\#10;d.offer("b");&\#10;d.push("c");&\#10;System.out.print(d.poll() + d.pop() + d.poll());&\#10;System.out.print(" " + d.peek());&\#10;System.out.print(" " + d.pop());</code></pre>{
~It prints <code>cab null null</code>, because <code>pop()</code> returns null on an empty deque.#Not quite. pop belongs to the throwing family with remove and element. Only poll and peek return null when the deque is empty.
=It prints <code>cab null</code>, then the last <code>pop()</code> throws <code>NoSuchElementException</code>.#Correct. push adds at the head and offer at the tail, so the deque is c, a, b. poll and pop both take the head. peek returns null on an empty deque, but pop throws.
~It prints <code>acb null</code>, then the last <code>pop()</code> throws <code>NoSuchElementException</code>.#Not quite. push("c") puts c at the head, in front of a, so the first poll returns c.
~It prints <code>cab</code>, then <code>peek()</code> throws <code>NoSuchElementException</code>.#Not quite. peek never throws; on an empty deque it returns null. The exception comes from the last pop.
}

::cs-final-q02::[html]<p>A report lists each word with the position where it was last seen. One map keeps first-seen order and one keeps sorted order. What does this print?</p><pre><code>List&lt;String&gt; words \= List.of("pear", "fig", "apple", "fig");&\#10;Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; first \= new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;();&\#10;Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; sorted \= new TreeMap&lt;&gt;();&\#10;for (int i \= 0; i &lt; words.size(); i++) \{&\#10;    first.put(words.get(i), i);&\#10;    sorted.put(words.get(i), i);&\#10;\}&\#10;System.out.println(first + " " + sorted);</code></pre>{
=<code>\{pear\=0, fig\=3, apple\=2\} \{apple\=2, fig\=3, pear\=0\}</code>#Correct. A repeated key replaces its value but keeps its place\: a LinkedHashMap keeps the order keys were first inserted, and a TreeMap sorts by key.
~<code>\{pear\=0, apple\=2, fig\=3\} \{apple\=2, fig\=3, pear\=0\}</code>#Not quite. Putting a key that is already present does not move it to the end of a LinkedHashMap; its insertion order is unchanged.
~<code>\{pear\=0, fig\=1, apple\=2\} \{apple\=2, fig\=1, pear\=0\}</code>#Not quite. put on an existing key replaces the old value, so fig ends with 3, the last position.
~<code>\{pear\=0, fig\=1, apple\=2, fig\=3\} \{apple\=2, fig\=1, fig\=3, pear\=0\}</code>#Not quite. A map holds one entry per key. A repeated key replaces the value instead of adding an entry.
}

::cs-final-q19::[html]<p>A stock map stores counts as <code>Integer</code> values, and no key is mapped to null. Where do boxing and unboxing happen on lines L1 to L5, and how does the code end?</p><pre><code>Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; stock \= new HashMap&lt;&gt;();&\#10;stock.put("pen", 3);                                    // L1&\#10;int pens \= stock.get("pen");                            // L2&\#10;Integer pads \= stock.get("pad");                        // L3&\#10;int total \= pens + stock.getOrDefault("pad", 0);        // L4&\#10;int padCount \= pads;                                    // L5</code></pre>{
=L1 boxes 3, L2 unboxes, L3 neither, and L4 boxes the default 0 and unboxes the result. L5 unboxes null and throws <code>NullPointerException</code>.#Correct. Values are boxed going into the map and unboxed when read into an int. L3 only stores the null reference in an Integer variable. L5 must unbox that null into an int, which throws.
~L1 boxes 3, L2 unboxes, and L3 unboxes the missing value and throws <code>NullPointerException</code>, so L4 and L5 never run.#Not quite. pads is an Integer, not an int, so L3 only stores the null reference. The unboxing that throws happens at L5.
~L1 boxes 3, L2 neither boxes nor unboxes because <code>get</code> returns an <code>int</code>, L3 neither, and L4 boxes 0. The code ends without error.#Not quite. A Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; returns Integer, so L2 unboxes. And L5 reads the null in pads into an int, which throws NullPointerException.
~L1 boxes 3, L2 unboxes, L3 neither, and L4 neither, because <code>getOrDefault</code> takes and returns an <code>int</code>. L5 unboxes null and throws <code>NullPointerException</code>.#Not quite. L5 is right, but the map holds Integer values, so the default 0 is boxed and the Integer result is unboxed for the addition.
}

::cs-final-q03::[html]<p>A stock map uses a record key with a deliberately weak hash code. What does this print, and which statement about <code>stock</code> holds?</p><pre><code>record Sku(String id) \{&\#10;    @Override public int hashCode() \{ return id.length(); \}&\#10;\}&\#10;Map&lt;Sku, Integer&gt; stock \= new HashMap&lt;&gt;();&\#10;stock.put(new Sku("A12"), 5);&\#10;stock.put(new Sku("B77"), 9);&\#10;System.out.println(stock.get(new Sku("B77")) + " " + stock.size());</code></pre>{
~<code>5 2</code>. With equal hash codes, <code>get</code> returns the first entry stored in the bucket.#Not quite. After the hash code chooses the bucket, get compares keys with equals, and only the B77 key is equal.
~<code>9 1</code>. The second <code>put</code> replaced the first entry, because the hash codes are equal.#Not quite. put replaces an entry only when the keys are equal. Equal hash codes alone are just a collision; both entries stay.
=<code>9 2</code>. Both keys have hash code 3, so they share a bucket and <code>equals</code> picks the B77 entry. The iteration order of <code>stock</code> is still not guaranteed.#Correct. The hash code only chooses the bucket; equals, which the record bases on id, finds the matching entry. HashMap never promises an iteration order.
~<code>9 2</code>. And because the hash codes are equal, iterating over <code>stock</code> always follows insertion order.#Not quite. The printed values are right, but HashMap gives no iteration-order guarantee, whatever the hash codes are. A LinkedHashMap keeps insertion order.
}

::cs-final-q04::[html]<p>Jobs are sorted for an on-call screen; some have no priority yet. What does this print?</p><pre><code>record Job(String team, Integer priority) \{\}&\#10;List&lt;Job&gt; jobs \= new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of(&\#10;    new Job("ops", 2), new Job("dev", null),&\#10;    new Job("dev", 1), new Job("ops", null)));&\#10;jobs.sort(Comparator.comparing(Job\:\:team)&\#10;    .thenComparing(Job\:\:priority, Comparator.nullsFirst(Comparator.naturalOrder()))&\#10;    .reversed());&\#10;jobs.forEach(j -&gt; System.out.print(j.team() + "\:" + j.priority() + " "));</code></pre>{
~<code>ops\:null ops\:2 dev\:null dev\:1</code>#Not quite. That reverses only the team key. reversed() at the end applies to the whole comparator built so far, so the priority order and the null position flip too.
=<code>ops\:2 ops\:null dev\:1 dev\:null</code>#Correct. Before reversed, the order is team A to Z with missing priorities first\: dev\:null dev\:1 ops\:null ops\:2. reversed() reverses that whole chain, including where the nulls go.
~<code>dev\:1 dev\:null ops\:2 ops\:null</code>#Not quite. That reverses only the priority key. reversed() is called on the whole chain, so the team order flips as well.
~It throws <code>NullPointerException</code> on the missing priorities.#Not quite. nullsFirst wraps the priority comparator, so null priorities are ordered instead of compared. A plain comparing on the null key would throw.
}

::cs-final-q25::[html]<p>A logger combines small functional pieces. What does this print?</p><pre><code>StringBuilder log \= new StringBuilder();&\#10;Consumer&lt;String&gt; shout \= s -&gt; log.append(s.toUpperCase());&\#10;Consumer&lt;String&gt; size \= s -&gt; log.append(s.length());&\#10;Predicate&lt;String&gt; isShort \= s -&gt; s.length() &lt; 4;&\#10;for (String w \: List.of("ox", "zebra", "cat"))&\#10;    if (isShort.negate().test(w)) shout.andThen(size).accept(w);&\#10;System.out.println(log);</code></pre>{
=<code>ZEBRA5</code>#Correct. negate keeps only words of 4 or more characters, so only zebra passes. andThen runs shout first, then size, on the same word.
~<code>OX2CAT3</code>#Not quite. Those are the short words. negate flips isShort, so the loop handles only words that are not short.
~<code>5ZEBRA</code>#Not quite. shout.andThen(size) runs shout first and size second.
~<code>ZEBRA</code>#Not quite. andThen returns a Consumer that runs both actions, so the length is appended after the upper-case word.
}

::cs-final-q05::[html]<p>A shop groups its orders by city. Cities repeat. What does this print?</p><pre><code>record Order(String city, String item) \{\}&\#10;List&lt;Order&gt; orders \= List.of(new Order("Oslo", "tea"), new Order("Rome", "tea"),&\#10;    new Order("Oslo", "jam"), new Order("Oslo", "tea"));&\#10;Map&lt;String, Long&gt; perCity \= orders.stream()&\#10;    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Order\:\:city, Collectors.counting()));&\#10;Map&lt;String, Set&lt;String&gt;&gt; items \= orders.stream()&\#10;    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Order\:\:city,&\#10;        Collectors.mapping(Order\:\:item, Collectors.toSet())));&\#10;System.out.println(perCity.get("Oslo") + " " + items.get("Oslo").size()&\#10;    + " " + perCity.get("Paris"));</code></pre>{
~<code>3 3 0</code>#Not quite. toSet drops the repeated tea, and groupingBy adds no entry with 0 for a missing city, so get returns null.
~<code>2 2 null</code>#Not quite. counting counts orders, not distinct items. Oslo has three orders.
~It throws <code>IllegalStateException</code>, because the key Oslo repeats.#Not quite. groupingBy is built for repeating keys. A toMap collector without a merge function is the one that throws on a repeated key.
=<code>3 2 null</code>#Correct. counting counts every Oslo order. mapping to a Set keeps tea once, so Oslo has 2 items. groupingBy makes no key for a city with no orders, so get returns null.
}

::cs-final-q29::[html]<p>In a coding screen you solve "find the most frequent word" with a frequency count. What is this solution's time complexity for n words?</p><pre><code>static String mostFrequent(List&lt;String&gt; words) \{&\#10;    Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; counts \= new HashMap&lt;&gt;();&\#10;    for (String w \: words) counts.merge(w, 1, Integer\:\:sum);&\#10;    String best \= null;&\#10;    for (Map.Entry&lt;String, Integer&gt; e \: counts.entrySet())&\#10;        if (best \=\= null || e.getValue() &gt; counts.get(best)) best \= e.getKey();&\#10;    return best;&\#10;\}</code></pre>{
~O(n squared)\: the second loop calls <code>counts.get</code> inside a loop.#Not quite. HashMap get is constant time on average, so calling it once per entry adds only O(n).
=O(n)\: two loops run one after the other, each at most n times, and <code>HashMap</code> <code>merge</code> and <code>get</code> are constant time on average.#Correct. The loops are sequential, not nested, so their costs add. The second loop visits at most n distinct words.
~O(n log n)\: a <code>HashMap</code> keeps its keys sorted, which costs log n per <code>merge</code>.#Not quite. HashMap keeps no order; sorted keys and log n operations belong to TreeMap.
~O(1)\: every map operation is constant time.#Not quite. Each operation is constant on average, but the code performs one per word, so the total grows with n.
}

::cs-final-q20::[html]<p><code>readings</code> is a <code>LinkedList&lt;Integer&gt;</code> of n values. This loop takes about 1 second when n is 20,000. About how long when the list doubles to 40,000 values?</p><pre><code>long total \= 0;&\#10;for (int i \= 0; i &lt; readings.size(); i++)&\#10;    total +\= readings.get(i);</code></pre>{
=About 4 seconds\: each <code>get(i)</code> on a <code>LinkedList</code> walks from the nearer end, which is O(n), so n calls make the loop O(n squared).#Correct. The loop runs n times and each get walks up to half the list, so doubling n multiplies the work by about 4.
~About 2 seconds\: a single loop over the list is linear.#Not quite. The loop is one pass, but every get(i) on a LinkedList walks node by node to reach index i, so each step costs O(n).
~About 2 seconds\: <code>get(i)</code> is constant time on any <code>List</code>.#Not quite. Constant-time get is documented for ArrayList. A LinkedList reaches index i by walking from the nearer end.
~About 8 seconds\: the walk inside <code>get</code> inside the loop makes the work cubic.#Not quite. One walk inside one loop gives n times n, which is quadratic, so doubling n gives about 4 times the work.
}

::cs-final-q06::[html]<p>A utility rounds numbers from one list into another. It must accept both calls in the comment. Which parameter types fit, and what happens to a wrong call?</p><pre><code>static void roundAll(List&lt;____&gt; in, List&lt;____&gt; out) \{&\#10;    for (Number n \: in)&\#10;        out.add((int) Math.round(n.doubleValue()));&\#10;\}&\#10;// roundAll(prices, totals);    prices\: List&lt;Double&gt;,  totals\: List&lt;Number&gt;&\#10;// roundAll(counts, anything);  counts\: List&lt;Integer&gt;, anything\: List&lt;Object&gt;</code></pre>{
~<code>in</code> is <code>List&lt;Number&gt;</code> and <code>out</code> is <code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code>.#Not quite. A List&lt;Double&gt; is not a List&lt;Number&gt;, and a List&lt;Number&gt; is not a List&lt;Integer&gt;, so the compiler rejects both calls.
~<code>in</code> is <code>List&lt;? super Number&gt;</code> and <code>out</code> is <code>List&lt;? extends Integer&gt;</code>.#Not quite. You cannot add to a list with an extends bound, and reading from a super-bounded list gives Object, not Number. The method body would not compile.
=<code>in</code> is <code>List&lt;? extends Number&gt;</code> and <code>out</code> is <code>List&lt;? super Integer&gt;</code>. A call that passes a <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> as <code>out</code> is rejected at compile time.#Correct. in is only read, so an extends bound accepts lists of any Number subtype. out is only written with Integer values, so a super bound accepts lists that can hold Integer. Type-safe generic code is checked by the compiler.
~<code>in</code> is <code>List&lt;? extends Number&gt;</code> and <code>out</code> is <code>List&lt;? super Integer&gt;</code>. A call that passes a <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> as <code>out</code> compiles, then fails with <code>ClassCastException</code> at run time.#Not quite. The bounds are right, but in type-safe code the compiler rejects that call. A ClassCastException at run time comes from raw or unchecked code.
}

::cs-final-q18::[html]<p>Old code adds values to a <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> through a raw alias, and the list is then passed to a newer generic method. What does the compiler infer for <code>T</code>, and what happens when the code runs?</p><pre><code>static &lt;T&gt; T firstOr(List&lt;T&gt; xs, T fallback) \{&\#10;    return xs.isEmpty() ? fallback \: xs.get(0);&\#10;\}&\#10;List&lt;String&gt; names \= new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();&\#10;List raw \= names;&\#10;raw.add("Ana");&\#10;raw.add(42);&\#10;String a \= firstOr(names, "none");&\#10;String b \= names.get(1);</code></pre>{
=<code>T</code> is <code>String</code>. The code compiles with unchecked warnings, <code>a</code> is Ana, and <code>String b \= names.get(1);</code> throws <code>ClassCastException</code> at run time.#Correct. T comes from List&lt;String&gt; and the String fallback. The raw alias let an Integer into the same list with only a warning; the compiler trusts names to hold String and inserts a cast when the element goes into String b, and that cast fails.
~<code>T</code> is <code>String</code>. The code does not compile, because <code>raw.add(42)</code> puts an <code>Integer</code> into a list of <code>String</code>.#Not quite. raw is a raw List, so the compiler only warns. That is exactly how unchecked code lets a wrong type in.
~<code>T</code> is <code>Object</code>, because the list was filled through raw code. The code runs without error, and <code>b</code> holds the text 42.#Not quite. names is declared List&lt;String&gt;, so T is String. Java never converts the Integer to a String; the inserted cast fails.
~<code>T</code> is <code>String</code>. The code compiles with unchecked warnings, but <code>firstOr</code> throws <code>ClassCastException</code>, because the list holds an <code>Integer</code>.#Not quite. firstOr returns element 0, which really is the String Ana. The failure comes only when the Integer is read into a String.
}

::cs-final-q07::[html]<p>In a small test log, home and cart each appear 5 times and <code>tie</code> prints. In the production log both appear 300 times, and <code>tie</code> does not print. What is the cause, and what is the repair?</p><pre><code>Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; hits \= new HashMap&lt;&gt;();&\#10;for (String page \: log)&\#10;    hits.merge(page, 1, Integer\:\:sum);&\#10;if (hits.get("home") \=\= hits.get("cart"))&\#10;    System.out.println("tie");</code></pre>{
~<code>merge</code> with <code>Integer\:\:sum</code> stops counting above 127. Replace it with <code>put(page, hits.get(page) + 1)</code>.#Not quite. merge counts correctly at any size. The suggested put would also throw NullPointerException for a page not seen before, because get returns null.
~HashMap stores counts above 127 in a different bucket. Read both counts with <code>getOrDefault</code> before comparing.#Not quite. Buckets depend on the key's hash code, not on the value. getOrDefault still returns Integer objects, so \=\= still compares references.
=<code>\=\=</code> compares two <code>Integer</code> objects by identity, and only values from -128 to 127 are guaranteed to be the same cached object. Compare with <code>Objects.equals(hits.get("home"), hits.get("cart"))</code>; the <code>merge</code> counting is correct.#Correct. get returns Integer objects, and \=\= between two objects compares references. Small values come from a cache, so the test passed by luck. equals compares the numbers.
~<code>\=\=</code> unboxes both counts to <code>int</code>, so the counts really differ. Rebuild the counts with <code>computeIfAbsent</code>.#Not quite. \=\= between two Integer objects does not unbox; it compares references. The counts are equal.
}

::cs-final-q08::[html]<p><code>Locker</code> overrides <code>equals</code> and <code>hashCode</code> correctly with <code>Objects.equals</code> and <code>Objects.hash</code>, using <code>block</code> and <code>number</code> only. A locker is changed while it is in a <code>HashSet</code>. Which statement is accurate?</p><pre><code>class Locker \{ String block; int number; String holder; /* equals, hashCode */ \}&\#10;&\#10;Set&lt;Locker&gt; taken \= new HashSet&lt;&gt;();&\#10;Locker l \= new Locker("C", 7, "Ana");&\#10;taken.add(l);&\#10;l.holder \= "Ben";   // change 1&\#10;l.number \= 8;       // change 2</code></pre>{
~Both changes break lookup, because any field change makes <code>l</code> a different object.#Not quite. Lookup depends only on the fields equals and hashCode use. holder is not one of them.
~Neither change breaks lookup, because <code>contains</code> receives the same reference that was added.#Not quite. HashSet first uses the current hash code to choose where to look. After change 2 that hash code differs from the one l was stored under.
~Change 2 breaks the symmetry rule of the <code>equals</code> contract, so <code>taken</code> may now hold <code>l</code> twice.#Not quite. Symmetry means a.equals(b) matches b.equals(a), and these methods still follow it. The problem is a stored key whose hash code changed.
=Change 1 is harmless, because <code>holder</code> is not used by <code>equals</code> or <code>hashCode</code>. Change 2 alters a field <code>hashCode</code> uses while <code>l</code> is stored, so from then on lookups for <code>l</code> can fail. Remove <code>l</code> before such a change and add it back after.#Correct. The set filed l under the hash code it had when added. Changing a field in equals and hashCode moves l's hash code, so lookups search the wrong place. Unrelated fields do not matter.
}

::cs-final-q21::[html]<p>Tag names use ASCII letters only and must be case-insensitive, but a <code>HashSet</code> of <code>Tag</code> sometimes holds both java and Java. Which rule does <code>Tag</code> break, and which repair keeps tags case-insensitive?</p><pre><code>class Tag \{&\#10;    final String name;&\#10;    Tag(String name) \{ this.name \= name; \}&\#10;    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) \{&\#10;        return o instanceof Tag t &amp;&amp; name.equalsIgnoreCase(t.name);&\#10;    \}&\#10;    @Override public int hashCode() \{ return name.hashCode(); \}&\#10;\}</code></pre>{
~Symmetry\: <code>a.equals(b)</code> can differ from <code>b.equals(a)</code>. Replace <code>equalsIgnoreCase</code> with <code>equals</code>.#Not quite. equalsIgnoreCase gives the same answer in both directions. Switching to equals would also make tags case-sensitive, which breaks the requirement.
~Equal objects must have equal hash codes. Replace the class with <code>record Tag(String name)</code>, which writes both methods.#Not quite. The rule is right, but a record's equals compares name exactly, so java and Java would become two different tags.
=Equal objects must have equal hash codes. Tag java equals Tag Java, but their hash codes differ. Store the name lower-cased once in the constructor, then compare it with <code>equals</code> and hash it with <code>hashCode</code>.#Correct. HashSet looks in the place the hash code picks, so two equal tags with different hash codes can both be stored. Once every Tag holds its name in one lower-case form, equals and hashCode use the same value, so equal tags always share a hash code.
~No rule is broken\: <code>hashCode</code> only has to return the same value each time it is called on one object.#Not quite. Consistency is one rule, but the contract also requires that equal objects have equal hash codes.
}

::cs-final-q09::[html]<p>The marked line does not compile. Which option repairs it, and what does <code>blocked</code> then accept?</p><pre><code>int minAge \= 18;&\#10;if (strict) minAge \= 21;&\#10;Predicate&lt;Person&gt; oldEnough \= p -&gt; p.age() &gt;\= minAge;   // does not compile&\#10;Predicate&lt;Person&gt; active \= Person\:\:active;&\#10;Predicate&lt;Person&gt; blocked \= oldEnough.and(active).negate();</code></pre>{
~Use a new local that is never reassigned, such as <code>int limit \= strict ? 21 \: 18;</code>, in the lambda. <code>blocked</code> then accepts only a person who fails both checks.#Not quite. The repair is right, but negating and gives true when either check fails, not only when both fail.
~Move the <code>if (strict)</code> line below the lambda. <code>blocked</code> then accepts a person who fails at least one of the two checks.#Not quite. minAge is still reassigned, just later, so it is not effectively final and the lambda still does not compile.
~Declare <code>final int minAge \= 18;</code> and keep the <code>if</code>. <code>blocked</code> then accepts a person who fails at least one of the two checks.#Not quite. A final local cannot be reassigned, so the if line stops compiling instead.
=Use a new local that is never reassigned, such as <code>int limit \= strict ? 21 \: 18;</code>, in the lambda. <code>blocked</code> then accepts a person who fails at least one of the two checks.#Correct. A lambda may use only effectively final locals, and limit is assigned once. negate flips the whole and, so blocked is true when the person is too young, inactive, or both.
}

::cs-final-q10::[html]<p>A reviewer asks which lambdas could become method references, and whether <code>Check</code> can be the target of a lambda. Which answer is right?</p><pre><code>interface Check \{ boolean test(int n); default Check not() \{ return n -&gt; !test(n); \} \}&\#10;&\#10;List&lt;Integer&gt; nums \= List.of("12", "7", "30").stream()&\#10;    .map(s -&gt; Integer.parseInt(s))                          // L1&\#10;    .filter(n -&gt; n &gt; 10)                                    // L2&\#10;    .toList();&\#10;Supplier&lt;List&lt;Integer&gt;&gt; fresh \= () -&gt; new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();    // L3&\#10;nums.forEach(n -&gt; System.out.println(n));                   // L4</code></pre>{
~L1 static, L2 unbound instance (<code>Integer\:\:compareTo</code>), L3 constructor, L4 bound instance. <code>Check</code> can be a lambda target.#Not quite. compareTo returns an int and needs a second Integer, while filter needs a boolean test against the fixed value 10. L2 cannot become a method reference.
=L1 static (<code>Integer\:\:parseInt</code>), L2 cannot become one, L3 constructor (<code>ArrayList\:\:new</code>), L4 bound instance (<code>System.out\:\:println</code>). <code>Check</code> can be a lambda target, because its default method does not count as abstract.#Correct. Each of L1, L3 and L4 only passes its parameters to one existing method or constructor. L2 compares with 10, which is not a method call. Check has exactly one abstract method.
~L1 static, L2 cannot become one, L3 constructor, L4 bound instance. <code>Check</code> cannot be a lambda target, because it declares two methods.#Not quite. Only abstract methods count. not() is a default method, so Check is still functional.
~L1 static, L2 cannot become one, L3 constructor, L4 unbound instance (<code>PrintStream\:\:println</code>). <code>Check</code> can be a lambda target.#Not quite. L4 always calls println on the one object System.out, so it is a bound reference. An unbound reference would need the PrintStream as a parameter.
}

::cs-final-q26::[html]<p>During a clean-up you replace each lambda with a method reference where one exists\: <code>s -&gt; s.isEmpty()</code>, <code>id -&gt; cache.get(id)</code>, <code>() -&gt; new HashSet&lt;String&gt;()</code> and <code>s -&gt; s.startsWith("\#")</code>. <code>cache</code> is a <code>Map</code> of <code>String</code> to <code>String</code>. Which answer is right?</p>{
~The <code>startsWith</code> lambda cannot become a method reference. The others become <code>String\:\:isEmpty</code> (unbound), <code>Map\:\:get</code> (unbound) and <code>HashSet\:\:new</code> (constructor).#Not quite. The lambda always calls get on the one object cache, so it becomes the bound reference cache\:\:get. Map\:\:get would need the map as a parameter.
~The <code>isEmpty</code> lambda cannot become a method reference, because <code>isEmpty</code> takes no argument. The others become <code>cache\:\:get</code>, <code>HashSet\:\:new</code> and <code>String\:\:startsWith</code>.#Not quite. A method that takes no argument is exactly what an unbound reference such as String\:\:isEmpty calls on its parameter. String\:\:startsWith cannot supply the fixed "\#".
=The <code>startsWith</code> lambda cannot become a method reference. The others become <code>String\:\:isEmpty</code> (unbound), <code>cache\:\:get</code> (bound) and <code>HashSet\:\:new</code> (constructor).#Correct. startsWith needs the extra argument "\#", which is not a lambda parameter. Each of the others only passes its parameters to one method or constructor.
~Every lambda can become a method reference, including <code>String\:\:startsWith</code> for the last one.#Not quite. String\:\:startsWith would need two inputs, the string and the prefix, but the lambda receives only s and fixes the prefix itself.
}

::cs-final-q16::[html]<p>A worker drains jobs from an <code>ArrayDeque</code> used as a stack. After the last job it crashes with <code>NoSuchElementException</code> instead of stopping. Which change makes it stop cleanly?</p><pre><code>while (true) \{&\#10;    Job next \= jobs.pop();&\#10;    if (next \=\= null) break;&\#10;    next.run();&\#10;\}</code></pre>{
~Keep <code>pop()</code>, and move the null check so it runs before <code>next.run()</code>.#Not quite. The null check already runs before run. The exception comes from pop itself, which never returns null.
~Replace <code>jobs.pop()</code> with <code>jobs.remove()</code>, which returns null on an empty deque.#Not quite. remove belongs to the throwing family with pop and element. It throws NoSuchElementException too.
~Switch to the legacy <code>Stack</code> class, which the Java API documentation recommends for stacks.#Not quite. The documentation recommends a Deque such as ArrayDeque over the legacy Stack class, and changing the class does not stop a throwing method from being called on an empty stack.
=Replace <code>jobs.pop()</code> with <code>jobs.poll()</code>. <code>poll</code> returns null on an empty deque and takes from the head, so the stack order stays last-in-first-out.#Correct. pop throws on an empty deque, so the null check is never reached. poll removes the same head element but returns null when nothing is left.
}

::cs-final-q17::[html]<p>A clean-up job must remove every expired <code>Session</code> from the same <code>sessions</code> list object while it scans it. In production <code>sessions</code> is an <code>ArrayList</code>. A unit test passes a list made with <code>List.of(...)</code> that contains an expired session. Which version is safe in production, and what happens in the test?</p><pre><code>// Version 1&\#10;for (Session s \: sessions) if (s.expired()) sessions.remove(s);&\#10;// Version 2&\#10;for (Iterator&lt;Session&gt; it \= sessions.iterator(); it.hasNext(); )&\#10;    if (it.next().expired()) it.remove();&\#10;// Version 3&\#10;sessions.stream().filter(s -&gt; !s.expired()).toList();</code></pre>{
~Version 2. It also works in the test, because the iterator removes the element itself.#Not quite. Version 2 is the safe choice in production, but a List.of list is unmodifiable, so any removal from it throws UnsupportedOperationException, even through the iterator.
~Version 1. In the test it throws <code>UnsupportedOperationException</code>.#Not quite. Calling the list's own remove inside a for-each loop over it can throw ConcurrentModificationException in production. Java detects this only on a best-effort basis, so never rely on it.
~Version 3. It works in both, because a stream never modifies the list.#Not quite. A stream never modifies its source, which is exactly the problem\: it builds a new list without the expired sessions and throws it away, so sessions keeps them.
=Version 2. In the test it throws <code>UnsupportedOperationException</code>, because a list made with <code>List.of</code> cannot be modified.#Correct. The iterator's own remove is the safe way to remove while scanning a modifiable list. A List.of list rejects every change, including a removal through its iterator.
}

::cs-final-q22::[html]<p><code>staff</code> holds three employees in two departments. This grouping prints a wrong size. What does it print, and what is the repair?</p><pre><code>Map&lt;String, List&lt;String&gt;&gt; byDept \= new HashMap&lt;&gt;();&\#10;for (Emp e \: staff)&\#10;    byDept.getOrDefault(e.dept(), new ArrayList&lt;&gt;()).add(e.name());&\#10;System.out.println(byDept.size());</code></pre>{
=<code>0</code>. <code>getOrDefault</code> returns the new list for a missing key but never stores it. Use <code>byDept.computeIfAbsent(e.dept(), d -&gt; new ArrayList&lt;&gt;()).add(e.name());</code>#Correct. Each name is added to a list that is never put in the map. computeIfAbsent creates the list, stores it under the key, and returns it.
~<code>2</code>. The code is already correct.#Not quite. getOrDefault only reads; nothing in the loop ever puts an entry into byDept.
~It throws <code>NullPointerException</code>, because <code>get</code> on a missing key returns null. Use <code>computeIfAbsent</code>.#Not quite. getOrDefault returns the default list, not null, so nothing throws. The map just stays empty.
~<code>2</code>, but each list keeps only the last name. Use <code>merge</code> with a new list.#Not quite. No entry is ever stored, so the size is 0, not 2.
}

::cs-final-q24::[html]<p><code>Score</code> defines its natural order by points. The board holds Ana 40, a null for a player who has not played yet, and Ben 25, and <code>board.sort(null)</code> throws <code>NullPointerException</code>. Which call sorts by the natural order with the missing entry last, and what order results?</p><pre><code>record Score(String name, int pts) implements Comparable&lt;Score&gt; \{&\#10;    public int compareTo(Score o) \{ return Integer.compare(pts, o.pts); \}&\#10;\}</code></pre>{
~<code>board.sort(Comparator.nullsLast(Comparator.naturalOrder()));</code> gives Ana 40, Ben 25, null.#Not quite. The call is right, but compareTo uses Integer.compare(pts, o.pts), so the natural order puts lower points first.
~<code>board.sort(Comparator.nullsLast(Comparator.comparing(Score\:\:name)));</code> gives Ana 40, Ben 25, null.#Not quite. That handles the null, but it sorts by name, a supplied ordering, not by the natural order on points.
=<code>board.sort(Comparator.nullsLast(Comparator.naturalOrder()));</code> gives Ben 25, Ana 40, null.#Correct. naturalOrder uses Score's compareTo, which puts fewer points first, and nullsLast places the null entry last instead of comparing it.
~<code>Collections.sort(board);</code> gives Ben 25, Ana 40, null.#Not quite. Collections.sort uses the natural order with no null handling, so it throws NullPointerException just like sort(null).
}

::cs-final-q11::[html]<p><code>rows</code> holds two invoices already loaded with JDBC to fill a screen's table, so the team filters them in Java rather than with a second query. What happens?</p><pre><code>Stream&lt;Invoice&gt; late \= rows.stream()&\#10;    .filter(i -&gt; \{ System.out.println("check " + i.id()); return i.daysLate() &gt; 30; \});&\#10;System.out.println("built");&\#10;List&lt;Invoice&gt; list \= late.toList();&\#10;Optional&lt;Invoice&gt; first \= late.findFirst();</code></pre>{
=<code>built</code> prints first, then one <code>check</code> line per invoice. Then <code>late.findFirst()</code> throws <code>IllegalStateException</code>, because the stream was already used.#Correct. filter is lazy, so nothing runs until the terminal toList. A stream can be used once; a second terminal operation throws IllegalStateException.
~One <code>check</code> line per invoice prints first, then <code>built</code>. Then <code>late.findFirst()</code> throws <code>IllegalStateException</code>.#Not quite. filter is an intermediate operation; it only describes work. The checks run when toList starts the pipeline, after built prints.
~<code>built</code>, then the <code>check</code> lines. Then <code>late.findFirst()</code> runs the filter again and prints the <code>check</code> lines a second time.#Not quite. A stream cannot be traversed twice. To run again, call rows.stream() for a new stream.
~<code>built</code>, then the <code>check</code> lines. Then <code>late.findFirst()</code> returns an empty <code>Optional</code>, because the stream is used up.#Not quite. A used stream does not quietly return empty. It throws IllegalStateException.
}

::cs-final-q28::[html]<p><code>readings</code> is a list of <code>Reading(String sensor, double value)</code> records, and sensor ids repeat. A chart needs every value for each sensor, in reading order, as a <code>Map&lt;String, List&lt;Double&gt;&gt;</code>. Which collector produces it?</p>{
=<code>Collectors.groupingBy(Reading\:\:sensor, Collectors.mapping(Reading\:\:value, Collectors.toList()))</code>#Correct. groupingBy collects every reading per sensor, and mapping keeps each value in encounter order in a list.
~<code>Collectors.toMap(Reading\:\:sensor, Reading\:\:value)</code>#Not quite. With no merge function, toMap throws IllegalStateException as soon as a sensor id repeats.
~<code>Collectors.toMap(Reading\:\:sensor, Reading\:\:value, (a, b) -&gt; a)</code>#Not quite. It no longer throws, but the merge keeps only the first value per sensor, and the values are Double, not lists.
~<code>Collectors.groupingBy(Reading\:\:sensor, Collectors.counting())</code>#Not quite. That gives the number of readings per sensor, not the values.
}

::cs-final-q30::[html]<p>A teammate wants to rewrite this loop as a stream pipeline over the loaded <code>orders</code> (<code>Order</code> records with <code>customer()</code> and <code>cents()</code>). Which pipeline produces the same strings in the same order? Ignore whether the returned list is modifiable.</p><pre><code>List&lt;String&gt; out \= new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();&\#10;for (Order o \: orders)&\#10;    if (o.cents() &gt; 1000) out.add(o.customer().toUpperCase());&\#10;Collections.sort(out);</code></pre>{
~<code>orders.stream().map(o -&gt; o.customer().toUpperCase()).filter(o -&gt; o.cents() &gt; 1000).sorted().toList()</code>#Not quite. After map the elements are Strings, which have no cents(), so this does not compile. Filter on the Order before mapping it.
=<code>orders.stream().filter(o -&gt; o.cents() &gt; 1000).map(o -&gt; o.customer().toUpperCase()).sorted().toList()</code>#Correct. filter keeps the same orders the if keeps, map does the same transformation, and sorted gives the natural String order that Collections.sort gives.
~<code>orders.stream().filter(o -&gt; o.cents() &gt; 1000).map(o -&gt; o.customer().toUpperCase()).toList()</code>#Not quite. The loop sorts its result; without sorted the pipeline keeps the orders' encounter order.
~<code>orders.stream().filter(o -&gt; o.cents() &lt;\= 1000).map(o -&gt; o.customer().toUpperCase()).sorted().toList()</code>#Not quite. filter keeps the elements its condition accepts, so this keeps exactly the orders the loop skips.
}

::cs-final-q12::[html]<p>Each order id has at most one invoice. A teammate proposes building a <code>Map&lt;String, Invoice&gt;</code> from <code>invoices</code> once, then calling <code>get(s.orderId())</code> inside a single loop over <code>shipments</code>. Both lists hold n records. Which assessment is right?</p><pre><code>for (Shipment s \: shipments)&\#10;    for (Invoice inv \: invoices)&\#10;        if (inv.orderId().equals(s.orderId())) s.attach(inv);</code></pre>{
~Before O(n squared); after O(n log n), because <code>HashMap</code> lookups are logarithmic. When both lists double\: about 4 times before, a little over 2 times after.#Not quite. HashMap get is constant time on average. Logarithmic lookups belong to TreeMap.
=Before O(n squared); after O(n), since building the map is one O(n) pass and each <code>HashMap</code> <code>get</code> is constant time on average. When both lists double\: about 4 times longer before, about 2 times after.#Correct. The nested loops do n times n comparisons. The map version does one pass to build and one pass to look up, so it grows linearly.
~Before O(n); after O(1). When both lists double\: about 2 times before, no change after.#Not quite. The original has a loop inside a loop, which is quadratic, and the new version still visits every shipment, which is linear.
~Before O(n squared); after still O(n squared), because building the map loops over the invoices once more.#Not quite. Building the map is a separate pass that runs once. Its cost adds to the loop's cost instead of multiplying it.
}

::cs-final-q13::[html]<p>A music app stores one playlist. Songs may repeat, the list keeps the user's order, new songs are added at the end, and the player often jumps straight to position i, for example after shuffle picks an index. Which implementation fits best, and why?</p>{
~<code>LinkedList</code>\: it keeps duplicates and order, and its fast add matters more than <code>get(i)</code>.#Not quite. Adding at the end is cheap in both lists, but LinkedList get(i) walks the nodes, which is linear, and the jumps are frequent.
~<code>LinkedHashSet</code>\: it keeps insertion order and has constant-time lookups.#Not quite. A set drops repeated songs and has no get by position.
~A <code>TreeMap</code> from position to song\: the keys stay sorted, so getting a position is constant time.#Not quite. TreeMap get is logarithmic, and positions would need renumbering whenever songs change.
=<code>ArrayList</code>\: it keeps duplicates and order, adding at the end is amortized constant time, and <code>get(i)</code> is constant time.#Correct. Every stated need is met, and the frequent jumps rely on constant-time access by index.
}

::cs-final-q23::[html]<p>A leaderboard must show every player sorted by score, highest first, and scores can tie. This version loses a player. Which fix meets the need?</p><pre><code>Map&lt;Integer, String&gt; board \= new TreeMap&lt;&gt;();   // score -&gt; player&\#10;board.put(90, "Ana");&\#10;board.put(75, "Ben");&\#10;board.put(90, "Cy");</code></pre>{
~Use a <code>LinkedHashMap</code> instead, so the map keeps insertion order.#Not quite. It still keeps one entry per score, so Cy still replaces Ana, and insertion order is not score order.
~Swap keys and values\: a <code>TreeMap</code> from player to score keeps every player and shows the highest score first.#Not quite. Every player is kept, but a TreeMap sorts by its key, so the board would be in name order, not score order.
=Keep a list of <code>Player(name, score)</code> records and sort it with <code>Comparator.comparing(Player\:\:score).reversed()</code>. A list keeps tied players.#Correct. A sorted list keeps duplicates, and the reversed comparator puts the highest score first. A map keyed by score keeps only one player per score.
~Use a <code>HashMap</code> from score to player; it keeps every entry and orders them by key.#Not quite. A HashMap also holds one entry per key, and it gives no ordering at all.
}

::cs-final-q14::[html]<p>A nightly report crashes with <code>NoSuchElementException\: No value present</code>, but only on nights when a region had no sales. Which diagnosis and repair are right?</p><pre><code>String topRep \= sales.stream()&\#10;    .filter(s -&gt; s.region().equals(region))&\#10;    .max(Comparator.comparing(Sale\:\:amount))&\#10;    .get()&\#10;    .rep();</code></pre>{
~<code>max</code> returns null for an empty stream, so <code>.rep()</code> fails. Check <code>topRep !\= null</code> after the pipeline.#Not quite. max never returns null; it returns an Optional. The symptom is NoSuchElementException from get, not NullPointerException.
=<code>max</code> returns an empty <code>Optional</code> when no sale matches, and <code>get()</code> throws on it. Replace <code>.get().rep()</code> with <code>.map(Sale\:\:rep).orElse("no sales")</code>.#Correct. map turns a present Sale into its rep and leaves an empty Optional empty, and orElse supplies the text for a night with no sales.
~<code>max</code> returns an empty <code>Optional</code> when no sale matches. Replace <code>.get()</code> with <code>.orElseThrow()</code>.#Not quite. The diagnosis is right, but orElseThrow with no argument throws the same NoSuchElementException, so the report still crashes.
~The filter throws when no sale matches the region. Wrap the filter call in try and catch.#Not quite. A filter that matches nothing just produces an empty stream. The exception comes from get on the empty Optional.
}

::cs-final-q27::[html]<p>Screen A loads today's orders as shown, to list them in a table, and also needs the total per region. Screen B shows only the number of late orders per region over the last two years, millions of rows. Where should each reduction happen?</p><pre><code>List&lt;Order&gt; today \= new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();&\#10;while (rs.next())&\#10;    today.add(new Order(rs.getString("region"), rs.getInt("cents")));</code></pre>{
~Both in SQL, so the database does all the work.#Not quite. Screen A already has today's rows in memory for its table. A second query for the totals adds a round trip without saving any loading.
=Screen A in a stream over <code>today</code>, because the rows are already loaded. Screen B in SQL with <code>WHERE</code> and <code>GROUP BY</code>, so only the counts come back.#Correct. Reduce in SQL when the program needs only the reduced result. Process in the stream when the rows are already loaded for another use.
~Both in streams\: load Screen B's rows the same way and group them with <code>groupingBy</code>.#Not quite. Screen B needs only counts, and loading millions of rows to count them wastes transfer and memory.
~Screen A in SQL and Screen B in a stream.#Not quite. That is the reverse of the rule\: A's rows are already loaded, and B needs only the reduced result.
}

::cs-final-q15::[html]<p>A coding screen asks\: given the keys a user typed, where \# means backspace (delete the previous character, if there is one), return the final text. Which pattern fits, and what is its cost for n keys?</p>{
~A <code>HashMap</code> frequency count of the characters. O(n).#Not quite. Counts lose the order of the characters, and the answer depends on order.
~A <code>HashSet</code> of seen characters. O(n).#Not quite. A set loses both repeats and order, so it cannot rebuild the text.
~Two pointers that start at both ends. O(n log n).#Not quite. Backspace depends on the most recent kept character, not on pairs from both ends, and two pointers alone would be O(n), not O(n log n).
=An <code>ArrayDeque</code> stack\: push each character, and on \# pop only if the stack is not empty. O(n).#Correct. Backspace always removes the most recent character still kept, which is last-in-first-out. Each key is pushed or popped once.
}
